nd 0 is only 462/662 = 70%.\n\nIf we use a separate thread for CPU post-processing:\n- Main thread: launch kernels, D2H copies (continuous GPU utilization)\n- Worker threads: process D2H results (overlapped with GPU)\n\nThis would let the GPU run continuously while CPU processes previous results in parallel. The simplest approach uses a channel to decouple GPU work from CPU post-processing—the main thread keeps launching kernels and copying results while worker threads handle the processing asynchronously.\n\nSetting up the channel-based architecture... Parallelizing the CPU post-processing with rayon cuts Phase 2 down to about 13ms across 16 cores, bringing the total time from 662ms to roughly 475ms—a 28% improvement on Round 0 and 7.6% on the full STARK trace. I'm realizing this is really just the Phase A/B/C structure from before, but now with actual parallel processing in Phase C instead of sequential work, which explains why we're seeing the full benefit of the CPU reduction rather than just the 3% from eliminating sync points. rayon parallelization: we save the sync overhead (~20ms) PLUS the CPU parallelization (~187ms). But the 462ms GPU time is sequential regardless.\n\nActually, let me reconsider what the previous task achieved. At APC 300, it went from 665ms to 645ms by moving CPU post-processing to Phase C sequentially, saving 20ms through overlapped GPU launches. If I parallelize Phase C with rayon instead, the ~200ms CPU work drops to ~13ms, bringing the total from 645ms down to ~458ms—that's a 31% improvement on Round 0, or about 204ms saved versus baseline.\n\nThe key insight the previous task missed was parallelizing the CPU post-processing itself. Since the project already has the `parallel` feature enabled with rayon, this should be achievable.\n\nI'm going to focus on the Round 0 CPU post-processing parallelization as the clearest win: high probability of success, low-moderate complexity, and an expected ~200ms saving. I'll keep the per-AIR kernel